# Architecture Rules

Full spec: `docs/architecture.md`. If code conflicts with it, change the code (or write an ADR).

## Layer Ownership

| Layer | Owns | Must Never |
|---|---|---|
| **Presentation** (`*.controller.ts`, `*.routes.ts`, `*.schemas.ts`, middleware) | HTTP parsing, validation, response serialization | Contain business logic, query Supabase, call provider SDKs |
| **Application** (`*.use-case.ts`, `*.dto.ts`, `*.policy.ts`) | Use-case orchestration, authorization, DTO contracts | Import Express, Supabase, or provider SDK types |
| **Domain** (`*.entity.ts`, `*.value-object.ts`, `*.repository.ts`, `*.queries.ts`) | Business invariants, state transitions, port interfaces | Import Express, Supabase, Pino, env vars, or any infrastructure package |
| **Infrastructure** (`supabase-*.repository.ts`, `*.mapper.ts`, provider adapters) | Supabase queries, row mapping, external provider calls | Define business rules, accept HTTP requests, bypass application authorization |

## Dependency Rule

```
Presentation → Application → Domain
Infrastructure → Domain (implements ports)
Presentation ✗ Infrastructure (never direct)
Domain ✗ any outer layer
```

Forbidden import pairs (hard reject):
- `*.use-case.ts` importing `createClient` or `@supabase/supabase-js`
- `*.entity.ts` or `*.value-object.ts` importing `express`, `Request`, or `Response`
- `*.controller.ts` calling `.from('table_name')`
- `*.use-case.ts` importing a concrete repository class (only interfaces allowed)
- One feature importing another feature's `infrastructure/` internals

## Folder Placement — Rule of Thumb (§18)

When adding new code, ask in order:
1. HTTP-specific (parsing, status codes, headers)? → **Presentation**
2. Orchestrating a workflow with no business rule of its own? → **Application**
3. A business rule, invariant, or calculation? → **Domain**
4. Persistence or external system call? → **Infrastructure**
5. Read for display/reporting, no invariant to protect? → **Application use case + Query Interface** (not command repo)

When in doubt, place closer to Domain than to the framework. It is cheaper to promote a rule outward later than to untangle it from Express or Supabase.

## Feature Module Anatomy

Every feature under `src/features/<name>/` must follow this structure:

```
features/<name>/
├── domain/          # entities, value objects, repository interface, query interface
├── application/     # use cases, DTOs, policies
├── presentation/    # controller, routes, Zod schemas
├── infrastructure/  # supabase repository impl, mapper
├── composition.ts   # wires repos + use cases; consumed only by composition-root
└── tests/
    ├── application/
    └── presentation/
```

`composition.ts` must NOT be imported by the feature's own domain, application, or presentation code — only by `src/app/composition-root.ts`.

## Repository Segregation (Logical CQRS)

Every feature exposes two separate interfaces — never one fat interface:

```ts
// Command repo — invariant-preserving lookups + save
interface MembershipRepository {
  findById(id: MembershipId): Promise<ClientMembership | null>;
  findActiveByClient(clientId: UserId): Promise<ClientMembership | null>;
  save(membership: ClientMembership): Promise<void>;
}

// Query interface — listing, filtering, reporting read-models
interface MembershipQueries {
  listExpiringSoon(criteria: ExpiringSoonCriteria, page: Pagination): Promise<Page<MembershipSummary>>;
  listRoster(criteria: RosterCriteria, page: Pagination): Promise<Page<MembershipSummary>>;
}
```

A command use case depends only on the command repo. A read use case depends only on the query interface.

## Hard-Reject Checklist (per layer)

**Presentation:** business logic inside controller · Supabase/SQL in route handler · Zod schema re-implementing a rule a Value Object already enforces (delegate with `superRefine` instead)

**Application:** direct Supabase/SQL in use case · same business rule duplicated across use cases · infrastructure SDK types leaking into use-case signatures · command use case depending on query interface or vice versa

**Domain:** imports from `express`, `@supabase/supabase-js`, `pino`, or any provider SDK · mutable Value Object or one whose constructor allows invalid state · Entity that is a pure data container (getters/setters, no invariant enforcement) · repository interface exposing SQL, table names, or row-shaped types

**Infrastructure:** business rules embedded in a repository · raw Supabase rows returned without mapping · adapter called directly from application/domain code, bypassing its port
